title: Why the log stops being a tree
tags: [[The operation log]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: The tree rested on three assumptions the watcher broke — few revisions, undo only of the last one, rollback to a state that existed — and its two mechanisms produced undo/redo bugs no rule could close.

The history tree was chosen on three assumptions (user, 12 October 2026). Each turned out
false:

* ''There would be few revisions'', so keeping months of them was reasonable. The watcher writes a revision per batch of filesystem events, and retention keeps only the last 200 (doc "Automatic retention"): the history is short, and a labelled state far back is not manageable.
* ''Undoing anything but the last operation would be rare.'' The watcher writes between any two things a user does, so "the change I want back" is almost never the last one.
* ''A rollback returns to a global state that existed.'' With the watcher's revisions interleaved, rewinding over them unrecords facts still true on disk (doc "Why undo does not step HEAD back"): the state reached is one the filesystem is not in.

!! What it cost in undo and redo

Undo chose between the two mechanisms: a rollback when the change was still the last
thing in the log, a revert otherwise. The choice depended on whether the watcher had
written yet — a timing nobody controls — and redo then had to guess which mechanism undo
had used and whether the world had moved since. Found while verifying the session runs
(doc "The session scenario", 12 October 2026):

* ''Redo lost after a rollback and a watcher write.'' Undo rolls back; the watcher's next flush (in the session: the GUI's input history file, written by the list's search) lands as a new child of HEAD; redo finds no continuation, steps over the watcher's revision and answers "Nothing to redo." The change is left on an abandoned branch, reachable only by a rollback that would unrecord the watcher's write. That was the run whose redo read 20 000 keys instead of 700 000.
* ''Redo stops at the first undo it already redid'' (`core::undo::plan_redo`, `rev.left.is_empty()` returns `Nothing` instead of stepping over it): two undos forced into reverts, then two redos — the second answers "Nothing to redo."
* ''The tagging scripts' going back'' (`mf_log_back_to` in `scripts/shipped/lib/mf-gui.sh`) rolls back to the head noted before an answer — over any watcher revision written meanwhile, which it unrecords.

Each could be patched — a fourth redo case re-applying an abandoned branch at HEAD, a
mechanism the daemon refuses on purpose. The rules were growing faster than the cases.
With one mechanism that never moves anything back, none of these cases exists.

!! Why revert can stand alone

The revert already covered the rollback: "a dependent-including revert ''is'' a rollback
to the target's parent, written forward" (doc "Operation dependencies"). What only the
tree gave was a global state that once existed — not needed, per the third point above.
